SOLUTIONS={
1:('DIG-01','One thousand is 1000 and twenty-seven is 27. Add them: 1000 + 27 = 1027. A zero is needed in the hundreds place.'),
2:('GEO-04','Viewing the lettering from behind the window gives a left-right mirror image. The word SHOP comes before PET, and each individual letter is also reflected. Only option E does both; merely reversing the order of otherwise normal letters is insufficient.'),
3:('ALG-01','First add the children’s ages: 14 + 10 = 24. Dad is twice this total, so he is 2 × 24 = 48 years old.'),
4:('GEO-02','Let the square have side s. The shaded triangle has base s and height s/2, so its area is ½ × s × s/2 = s²/4. The whole square has area s²; the shaded fraction is 1/4.'),
5:('LOGIC-02','Build the order from the clues. Tim is ahead of Carl, who is ahead of Sarah. Sarah is ahead of Brett, who is ahead of George. Thus the order is Tim, Carl, Sarah, Brett, George: Brett is fourth.'),
6:('ALG-03','The replacement increases one number by 532 − 235 = 297. The total therefore increases by exactly 297: 2010 + 297 = 2307. There is no need to know the other four numbers.'),
7:('SOLID-03','Count joins, then count the two faces in each join. There are three joins along each row of four blocks and three joins between the offset rows. That makes 3 + 3 + 3 = 9 joins. Each joins two faces, so 18 faces are glued.'),
8:('DIG-02','Use descending order for the largest number, 752, and ascending order for the smallest, 257. Their difference is 752 − 257 = 495.'),
9:('FRAC-01','Putting one-fifth in the bank leaves 4/5 × $1000 = $800. One-quarter of this remainder is $200. Mother receives the rest: $800 − $200 = $600. The quarter is of $800, not of the original $1000.'),
10:('GEO-01','All the short horizontal steps together equal the bottom width, 13 m. All the short downward steps together equal the left height, 12 m. The perimeter is therefore 13 + 12 + 13 + 12 = 50 m.'),
11:('RATE-01','Count only class time. From 9:10 to 11:00 is 110 minutes; 11:30 to 1:00 is 90 minutes; 1:50 to 3:00 is 70 minutes. Total: 110 + 90 + 70 = 270 minutes.'),
12:('ALG-02','Two numbers with average 11 lie equally far either side of 11. Their difference is 6, so each is 3 away from the average. The larger is 11 + 3 = 14; the smaller is 8.'),
13:('GEO-02','The rectangle’s width is 2 + 4 = 6. If its height is h, its area is 6h. The shaded trapezium has parallel sides 3 and 2, so its area is (3 + 2)h/2 = 2.5h. Its fraction of the rectangle is 2.5/6 = 5/12.'),
14:('COUNT-04','The intended reading is that every one of the 55 students takes at least one of these two subjects. Counting enrolments gives 39 + 35 = 74; each student taking both has been counted once too many. Thus 74 − 55 = 19 take both. Wording note: if students taking neither were allowed, 19 would be only the minimum, not a uniquely determined answer.'),
15:('GEO-02','Use the smallest triangles as a common area unit. One hexagon contains 6 of them. The large triangle has rows containing 1, 3, 5, 7 and 9, a total of 25. Its area in hexagonal units is 25/6 = 4⅙.'),
16:('GEO-01','A border of equal width removes the same total amount from the length and the width. The outer dimensions differ by 120 − 90 = 30 cm, so the inner dimensions must also differ by 30 cm. Only 90 by 60 does. It corresponds to a border (120 − 90)/2 = 15 cm wide.'),
17:('LOGIC-02','The three mutually joined left-hand circles must contain 1, 3 and 5, because no pair there may be consecutive. One valid arrangement puts X = 3, upper middle = 1, lower middle = 5, Y = 4 and lower right = 2. Check every joined pair: their differences are at least 2. This gives X + Y = 3 + 4 = 7, which is one of the choices.'),
18:('GEO-02','Count the garden in square-block units. The whole rectangle is 7 by 5, or 35 blocks. Each grass patch is 2 by 3, so the two patches cover 12 blocks. These 12 blocks have area 108 m², making each block 9 m². The path covers 35 − 12 = 23 blocks: 23 × 9 = 207 m².'),
19:('FRAC-02','The morning sales were $720 ÷ $12 = 60 hats. Twice as many after lunch means 120 hats, earning 120 × $9 = $1080. The day’s total is $720 + $1080 = $1800.'),
20:('GEO-02','The two right rectangles have the same width, so the lower height is 20/25 = 4/5 of the upper height. The left rectangles have the same width too. Their areas have that same ratio, giving shaded area 70 × 4/5 = 56 cm².'),
21:('DIG-01','Let the digits be a and b. Adding 10a + b and 10b + a gives 11(a + b) = 132, so a + b = 12. Valid digit pairs give 39, 48, 57, 66, 75, 84 and 93: seven possible original numbers.'),
22:('ALG-03','Write B, S and N for the weights of a bolt, screw and nail. Cancelling a bolt from 2B + 2S = B + 10N gives B + 2S = 10N. Also B = S + N. Substitute to get 3S + N = 10N, so S = 3N and B = 4N. One bolt weighs four nails.'),
23:('GEO-05','Pair the earliest and latest birthdays. For any date between 1 November and 1 December, the total distance to those two dates stays the same. The same is true for the pair 12 November and 21 November while inside that interval. The remaining middle birthday is 18 November, so choosing 18 November makes its distance zero without increasing either paired total. The median minimises total distance.'),
24:('DATA-02','The drawn columns represent 2 families with no cars, 8 with one and 2 with three: 12 families and 14 cars. Let x families have two cars. The average condition is (14 + 2x)/(12 + x) = 4/3. Thus 42 + 6x = 48 + 4x, giving x = 3. Check: 20 cars among 15 families gives 1⅓ cars each.'),
25:('ALG-01','Let the original width be w. Its perimeter is 2(12 + w). The wider rectangle has perimeter 2(12 + 5w), which equals twice the original perimeter. Hence 12 + 5w = 24 + 2w, so 3w = 12 and w = 4. The new rectangle is 12 by 20 and has perimeter 2(12 + 20) = 64 cm.')}
